TY  - JOUR
ID  - CIT-001
TI  - Community health worker-led versus facility-based type 2 diabetes care in rural Lesotho: a cluster-randomized trial within the ComBaCaL cohort study
AU  - Gerber, Felix
AU  - Gupta, Ravi
AU  - Labhardt, Niklaus Daniel
AU  - Amstutz, Alain
AB  - BACKGROUND: Type 2 diabetes poses a growing public health burden in low- and middle-income countries, where major gaps in access to chronic care persist. Task-shifting to Community Health Workers (CHWs) and the use of digital clinical decision support systems may bridge these gaps. Randomized trials evaluating CHW-led care models in which CHWs initiate, titrate, and monitor first-line diabetes treatment are lacking. METHODS: We conducted a cluster-randomised trial nested within the ComBaCaL (Community-Based Chronic Care Lesotho) cohort (NCT05596773). The cohort spans 103 rural villages in Lesotho, managed by trained, supervised CHWs. After home-based screening of cohort participants ≥40 years old or with body mass index ≥25 kg/m2, all with type 2 diabetes were enrolled in the trial. In intervention villages, CHWs - guided by a tablet-based clinical decision support system - provided care, including initiation and monitoring of metformin, atorvastatin and aspirin. In control villages, participants were referred to facility-based care.
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1186/s12916-026-04943-4
JO  - BMC Medicine
ER  - 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - CIT-002
TI  - Effect of Vitamin C on bleeding and pain after kidney biopsy: a randomized controlled trial
AU  - Li, Jipeng
AU  - Zhao, Fang
AU  - Lu, Rui
AU  - He, Lijie
AB  - BACKGROUND: Percutaneous renal biopsy (PRB) is an essential diagnostic procedure for kidney diseases, yet postbiopsy perirenal hematoma and pain remain frequent complications. METHODS: This single-center, prospective, open-label, randomized controlled trial enrolled 710 adults with chronic kidney disease (CKD) scheduled for an ultrasound-guided native kidney biopsy. Participants were randomly assigned (1:1) to receive perioperative intravenous vitamin C (20 mg/kg at 6 h before and 18 h after biopsy) or standard care alone. RESULTS: The incidence of any perirenal hematoma was 56.3% in the vitamin C group versus 50.1% in the control group (risk ratio, 1.12; 95% CI, 0.98–1.29; P = 0.098). CONCLUSIONS: In CKD patients undergoing PRB, perioperative intravenous vitamin C did not reduce the size of perirenal hematoma, alleviate postbiopsy pain, or decrease the incidence of bleeding complications within 24 h.
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1186/s12916-026-05037-x
JO  - BMC Medicine
ER  - 
TY  - DBASE
ID  - NCT05596773
TI  - Community-Based Chronic Disease Care in Rural Lesotho: The ComBaCaL Cohort Study
AU  - University Hospital, Basel, Switzerland
AB  - This ComBaCaL cohort study is to assess the impact of community-based, lay-led chronic disease screening and care interventions in rural Lesotho. It aims to establish a prospective research and service delivery platform in rural Lesotho that is managed by eHealth-supported Chronic Care Village Health Worker (CC-VHWs) providing regular chronic disease screening, monitoring and referral services. Subsequently, nested trials to assess the effectiveness of specific chronic disease control interventions will be developed. Trial registration: NCT05596773.
PY  - 2023
